= Twenty Desks, No More

Razem's education programme makes twenty pupils the maximum, not a target that
disappears when a municipality lacks rooms or teachers. The ministry agrees
smaller classes are healthier and warns that a ceiling without buildings and
salaries will be enforced by timetable fiction.

- @razem_desks_twenty
- @razem_desks_early_years
- @razem_desks_refuse

@razem_desks_twenty
title: Fund the twenty-pupil ceiling nationwide
subtitle: Cost: 4 budget capacity. Public education gains staff, space and a visible standard; recruitment becomes urgent.
choose-if: budget >= 4
unavailable-subtitle: Teachers, classrooms and municipal capital need four budget points.
on-arrival: budget -= 4; fiscal_stress += 3; public_debt += 2; razem_strength += 6; razem_dissent -= 10; public_sector_support += 8; young_support += 6; women_support += 4; rural_support += 3; household_security += 3; health_capacity += 3; administrative_capacity += 4; government_delivery += 5; government_goal_welfare = 1; public_trust += 5; market_liberal_dissent += 4; government_coalition_dissent += 3; news_headline = "Every public-school class receives a twenty-pupil legal ceiling"

The state finances teachers and rooms rather than ordering municipalities to
divide the same resources more finely. Families receive a number they can count
at the classroom door.

